WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.937
The second guided practice moves into DML.

00:00:02.937 --> 00:00:07.057
You will insert a new patient using a unique medical record number,

00:00:07.057 --> 00:00:14.612
use RETURNING to see what PostgreSQL stored, verify the row with a key-based SELECT, and then update the age.

00:00:14.612 --> 00:00:18.927
Work through those steps in order rather than trying to write everything at once.

00:00:18.927 --> 00:00:23.882
After the insert, inspect the generated identifier and the values you supplied.

00:00:23.882 --> 00:00:28.137
Then use the unique key to verify that you are looking at the intended row.

00:00:28.137 --> 00:00:31.447
Before the update, preview that same row again.

00:00:31.447 --> 00:00:34.032
After the update, inspect RETURNING.

00:00:34.032 --> 00:00:37.577
The purpose is to make safe verification feel routine.

00:00:37.577 --> 00:00:44.467
When data changes, always know which row you expected to change and confirm that PostgreSQL changed that row.

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:48.272
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:48.272 --> 00:00:53.347
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:53.347 --> 00:00:56.877
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:56.877 --> 00:01:01.232
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:01.232 --> 00:01:08.317
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:14.687
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:14.687 --> 00:01:16.966
not only whether the statement executes.
